::ALG2_U02_L08_GP01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L08_GP01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>Which polynomial has both ends rising? {
= f(x) = 2x<sup>4</sup> - x + 1#Correct. Even degree and positive leading coefficient make both ends rise.
~ f(x) = -2x<sup>4</sup> + 1#Even degree with a negative leading coefficient makes both ends fall.
~ f(x) = x<sup>3</sup> - 1#Odd degree has opposite end behavior.
~ f(x) = -x<sup>3</sup> + 1#Odd degree has opposite end behavior.
}

::ALG2_U02_L08_GP02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L08_GP02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What does an even multiplicity zero usually do on a graph? {
~ The graph crosses the x-axis.#Crossing is typical of odd multiplicity.
= The graph touches and turns at the x-axis.#Correct. Even multiplicity commonly causes a bounce.
~ The graph has no x-intercept.#A zero is an x-intercept when it is real.
~ The graph becomes linear.#Multiplicity does not make the whole graph linear.
}

::ALG2_U02_L08_GP03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L08_GP03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.4<br><br>Question:<br>A quadratic model has vertex (3, -5) and opens upward. What is the minimum value? {
~ 3#The x-coordinate gives where the minimum occurs.
~ -3#This is not the y-value of the vertex.
= -5#Correct. The y-coordinate of the vertex is the minimum value.
~ 5#The sign of the y-value matters.
}

::ALG2_U02_L08_GP04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L08_GP04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which interval solves x<sup>2</sup> - 4 >= 0? {
~ -2 <= x <= 2#That is where the graph is on or below the x-axis.
~ -2 < x < 2#This interval excludes zeros and has the wrong sign.
~ all real numbers#The graph is negative between the zeros.
= x <= -2 or x >= 2#Correct. The graph is on or above the x-axis outside the zeros.
}

::ALG2_U02_L08_GP05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L08_GP05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only application; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>A polynomial model has an x-intercept at 6 hours. What can that mean in context? {
~ The model is always increasing after 6 hours.#An intercept does not guarantee increasing behavior.
= The output quantity is 0 at 6 hours.#Correct. An x-intercept means the function value is 0.
~ The maximum value is 6.#An x-intercept is not necessarily a maximum.
~ The leading coefficient is 6.#An intercept does not identify the leading coefficient.
}

